ot  it's a different story but they just  won't even register you so you can stay  there so you're forced to leave anyway  but when you get the feeling that people  are starting to like make phone calls  and then you start to see police  officers and things like this you know  it's time to leave because the times  that we did stick around we've been told  by police officers that you actually  have to get on your bike and just get  the hell out of there wow and what we  notice sometimes is when we would be in  these areas later on we would see  actual police officers sometimes in  notepads or their phones  at certain kind of designated points on  the road from the next from where we  just left to the next city so we'd be  monitored the entire time and you  realize that you know especially when  you travel around rural china  you realize that it's very much akin to  the security apparatus that maybe like a  place like north korea would have it's  just not as in your face and it has more  money right  so you're minded the entire time and  ultimately we found out really how bad  it can get when we were up in inner  mongolia and you know our hotel room was  just full of swat team people  with body cameras and automatic weapons  that's when you realize that i'm here  trying to film some horses and talk to  some local people about their way of  life and then there's a swat team and  detectives in my hotel room  interrogating me about my every movement  in the entire country  um now when something like that happened  after they're done interrogating you do  they leave and then you just kind of  finish what you were doing  no that's that's the worst part is that  there's a a system of  i don't know i think they want to  instill some sort of paranoia because  what they'll do  is uh i'll use the example in the hotel  room with the swat team what they'll do  is be like  okay we didn't really get what we wanted  you know they they list off where we  work what our contracts were 