specifically  in the context of the uyghurs and we  interviewed jeffrey kane about his book  and he you know writes specifically  about how it's sometimes used uh to  restrict the movement of the weaker  people and in other ways can you talk a  little more generally just about how for  the average person living in china and  maybe there is no such thing maybe it's  very different urban versus rural or  whatever the case may be sure how does  that system affect the average person  yeah so the social credit system is i've  done a huge uh  basically a documentary series on the  whole thing  what it is is it's more of a dynamic  thing that's being tested out so i think  a lot of people you know i wanted to go  out there and dispel this myth that it's  some sort of number that hangs over your  head like a black mirror or something  it's not it's not that at all in fact it  was it was rolled out in this place  called rongchang in shandong in northern  china  and it was the first place that they  they rolled it out and it's no secret in  fact i found the  government documents that are out there  in public and i translated them myself  and that's what i based my video on  and what it did was teach me how it  works you know from a you know a test  standpoint testing out on this city and  its people which it's mandatory there  but it's not necessarily something  that's used elsewhere right there are  other cities in fact i reached out to a  lot of people around china to ask them  if they even knew about it i would say a  good chunk of people hadn't even heard  about it but then there were there were  cities there were other cities and like  in chengdu and then i talked to people  in anhui i talked to people all over  china that did know about it in fact  their city had some sort of version of  it and what it is it's really trying to  reward good behavior  with a point-based system and then  punish bad behavior and unfortunately  when i went through those documents and  i spoke to people around china  it  looks like it's trying to reward good  societal behavior which is probably a  fine it's a fine thing it's like uh you  know yeah i don't have a criminal record  so my score goes higher right but  unfortunately a good proportion of those  crimes of those things that lower your  score  are things about speaking out against  the government about posting uh messages  of dissent on on forums or on on chat  groups um you know uh having  weird religious beliefs as they call it  you know cult-like religious beliefs it  turns out that a lot of it is aimed at  reducing personal freedom  so that people don't dissent against the  government  once you're sort of branded or  stigmatized or assigned you know a  lesser status whether it's specifically  point-based or not  how do you recover from that how can you  how can you get back to sort of being on  on everyone's good side so to speak  well unfortunately if you're on like the  low  this is what i pointed out in my in my  video series is that when i spoke to  people there's no  even though there's a sliding scale of  points if the government deems you the  lowest score you just get the lowest  score there's no there's no rule of law  or rhyme or reason in this i think  people really rely on this western lens  of like everything works to a certain  standard everything works because of  rules and laws that's not really how it  works like if china wants to throw you  the lowest score and make you like  ostracize in society they'll just do  that right off the bat right especially  if it's something about the government  or destroy you in jail but if a person  is like labeled at the bottom i mean  your your kids probably aren't going to  get into schools right um you know  you're not going to be able to get  employment opportunities at least above  the lowest you know blue collar kind of  construct construction type jobs even if  you're well educated um  and to get out of that if you're at the  bottom y